==========================================
DAY 15 NOTES — Text Processing: grep & sed
Date: 12-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- grep -i = case-insensitive, grep -v = invert match (exclude), grep -n = show line numbers
- grep -c counts matches, grep -r searches recursively through folders
- Basic regex: ^ = starts with, $ = ends with, . = any character, [0-9] = any digit
- sed 's/old/new/' by DEFAULT just prints the result — doesn't touch the original file
- sed -i actually edits the file in place (no undo!) — ALWAYS test without -i first
- sed -i.bak edits the file but keeps a backup copy automatically
- sed 's/old/new/g' — the 'g' means replace ALL matches per line, not just the first

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] grep -i / -v / -n / -c
[ ] grep with basic regex (^ and $)
[ ] sed 's/old/new/' (preview mode)
[ ] sed -i.bak (safe in-place editing)
[ ] sed '/pattern/d' (deleting lines)

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ grep -i "error" logfile.txt
> 

$ sed 's/old/new/' notes.txt (preview, no -i)
> 

$ sed -i.bak 's/old/new/g' notes.txt
> 


